CurlFtpFS
=========

CurlFtpFS mounts an FTP or FTPS server as a local filesystem. This version is
implemented in Rust and uses the `fuser` FUSE library and the `curl` crate
(libcurl) for transport. It reconnects on each operation, so an FTP server
closing an idle control connection does not invalidate the mount.

Requirements
------------

* Rust 1.70 or newer
* libcurl development files
* A kernel with FUSE support (`fusermount3` is normally supplied by fuse3)

Build and install
-----------------

    cargo build --release
    install -Dm755 target/release/curlftpfs /usr/local/bin/curlftpfs
    install -Dm644 doc/curlftpfs.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/curlftpfs.1

Usage
-----

    curlftpfs [options] <ftp-host-or-url> <mountpoint>

For example:

    curlftpfs -o user=alice:secret,allow_other ftp.example.org /mnt/ftp

Run `curlftpfs --help` for the compatibility options. Existing curlftpfs
options are accepted with the traditional comma-separated `-o` syntax,
including passive/active mode, TLS certificates, proxies, address selection,
and metadata cache settings. Standard FUSE mount options are passed to FUSE.

Filesystem behavior
-------------------

Directory metadata is obtained with MLSD by default, with automatic fallback
to traditional Unix or Windows `LIST` formats for older servers. Reads are downloaded
through libcurl. A writable file handle is buffered and uploaded on flush or
close, which preserves random-write behavior expected by editors while working
within FTP's whole-object upload model. Create, truncate, mkdir, unlink, rmdir,
rename, chmod, readlink, fsync, and statfs operations are translated or
synthesized using the same FTP-compatible behavior as the original program.

FTP does not provide all POSIX semantics. Ownership and permissions are
therefore synthesized locally, and concurrent writers to one remote path use
last-close-wins behavior.

Testing
-------

    cargo test
    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings

